{"content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.4cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs}\n\\usepackage[capitalize]{cleveref}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\\newcommand{\\vt}{v_2}\n\\title{The Universal Ghost Clock:\\\\ repulsion, ride caps, and the census budget\\\\\n\\large with a verified import audit of arXiv:2603.11066}\n\\author{John A. Janik\\\\ \\small\\texttt{john.janik@gmail.com}}\n\\date{June 2026}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nWe generalize the two-clock structure of the companion note to\n\\emph{every} periodic ghost at once: for any valuation signature\n$\\sigma=(k_1,\\dots,k_\\ell)$ of depth $K=\\sum k_i$, the phantom root\n$\\rho_\\sigma=C_\\sigma/(2^K-3^\\ell)$ carries an exact clock --- alignment\n$a(x)=\\vt(x-\\rho_\\sigma)$ drops by exactly $K$ per ridden block, the\nride lasts exactly $\\lfloor(a-1)/K\\rfloor$ blocks, and positivity caps\n$a\\le\\log_2(|2^K-3^\\ell|\\,x+C_\\sigma)$. The $-1$ and $+1$ clocks are\nthe $K=1,2$ instances; the $(1,1,2)$-channel ghost $-19/11$ of the\nTarget-2 analysis is the $K=4$ instance, which resolves the question\nleft open there: \\emph{the parity channel is metered exactly like the\n$-1$ ghost}. The without-replacement depth supply extends verbatim to\nevery phantom root. These statements were prompted by the manuscript\narXiv:2603.11066v6 (Chang), whose relevant pieces we audited: the\nrepulsion mechanism, the census counting formula (after restoring a\nmissing hypothesis), and the amortized census constant\n$R\\le0.0893$ all \\emph{verify} (we recompute $R=0.08824$\nindependently); its burst--gap calculus is an exact relabeling of our\ntwo clocks (its Persistent Exit Lemma is our parity handoff at $h=3$);\nbut its shadow return-time theorem is \\emph{false as stated} ---\nre-entry gaps of $1$ occur, and 2-adic alignment can jump $+16$ bits\nin one step --- and must be replaced by the supply bound.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Setup}\n\nA \\emph{signature} is a word $\\sigma=(k_1,\\dots,k_\\ell)$, $k_i\\ge1$,\nof depth $K=\\sum k_i$; write $B_j=k_1+\\cdots+k_j$. The block map is\n$F_\\sigma(x)=(3^\\ell x+C_\\sigma)/2^K$ with\n$C_\\sigma=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}3^{\\ell-1-j}2^{B_j}$, and its unique fixed\npoint is the \\emph{phantom root}\n\\[\n\\rho_\\sigma=\\frac{C_\\sigma}{2^K-3^\\ell}\\in\\mathbb Z_2,\n\\]\na $2$-adic unit ($C_\\sigma$ odd, $2^K-3^\\ell$ odd). Define the\n\\emph{alignment} $a(x)=\\vt\\bigl((2^K-3^\\ell)x-C_\\sigma\\bigr)\n=\\vt(x-\\rho_\\sigma)$. Our catalogue of ghosts is exactly the family of\nphantom roots:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\sigma & \\ell & K & \\rho_\\sigma & \\text{role}\\\\\n\\midrule\n(1) & 1 & 1 & -1 & \\text{the $-1$ clock (ascent meter)}\\\\\n(2) & 1 & 2 & +1 & \\text{the $+1$ clock (trivial cycle)}\\\\\n(1,2) & 2 & 3 & -5 & \\text{negative cycle}\\\\\n(1,1,2) & 3 & 4 & -19/11 & \\text{the parity channel of Target 2}\\\\\n(1,1,1,2,1,1,4) & 7 & 11 & -17 & \\text{negative cycle}\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n\\section{The universal clock}\n\nAll verification counts below refer to \\texttt{search/ghostclock.py},\nrun over the five catalogued families plus $20$ random signatures.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[Cylinder threshold]\\label{lem:cyl}\n$a(x)\\ge K+1$ if and only if the next $\\ell$ letters of $x$ are\nexactly $\\sigma$; the modulus $2^{K+1}$ is minimal. (Verified: minimal\nforcing modulus $=K+1$ for all $25$ signatures.)\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Universal ghost clock]\\label{thm:clock}\nLet $a=a(x)\\ge K+1$. Then:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[(i)] (\\emph{Repulsion.}) After one ridden block,\n$F_\\sigma(x)-\\rho_\\sigma=\\tfrac{3^\\ell}{2^K}(x-\\rho_\\sigma)$, so the\nalignment drops by exactly $K$. (Verified: $200$ block applications,\ndrop $=K$ exactly.)\n\\item[(ii)] (\\emph{Ride cap.}) The orbit rides exactly\n$\\lfloor(a-1)/K\\rfloor$ consecutive $\\sigma$-blocks and then deviates.\n(Verified: $625$ cases, equality in all.)\n\\item[(iii)] (\\emph{Height cap.}) For positive integers $x$,\n$2^a\\le|(2^K-3^\\ell)x-C_\\sigma|$, so\n$a\\le\\log_2\\bigl(|2^K-3^\\ell|\\,x+C_\\sigma\\bigr)$: deep alignment costs\nheight, uniformly in $\\sigma$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\end{theorem}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n(i) is one line from the affine form of $F_\\sigma$. For (ii): each\nridden block requires alignment $\\ge K+1$ at its start\n(\\cref{lem:cyl}) and costs exactly $K$, so the number of ridden blocks\nis $\\#\\{j\\ge0:a-jK\\ge K+1\\}=\\lfloor(a-1)/K\\rfloor$; if the block were\nfollowed at alignment $<K$, (i) would force negative valuation of a\nnonzero integer, and at alignment exactly $K$ the deviating bit sits\nprecisely where the final letter's valuation is read, so the last\nletter strictly exceeds $k_\\ell$. (iii) is positivity of the nonzero\ninteger $(2^K-3^\\ell)x-C_\\sigma$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{corollary}[The parity channel is metered]\\label{cor:parity}\nThe $(1,1,2)^\\infty$ channel --- natural depth law, pure parity bias,\nthe configuration shown in the companion note to evade the $g$-law\nalone --- is governed by the $K=4$ clock of $\\rho=-19/11$: riding $j$\nblocks requires alignment $\\ge4j+1$, alignment is repelled at $4$ bits\nper block, and alignment $a$ at height $x$ requires\n$11x+19\\ge2^a$. The question left open in the two-clock note is\nresolved: \\emph{every} ghost channel, not just $-1$, carries an exact\nrun-length cap.\n\\end{corollary}\n\n\\begin{lemma}[Universal depth supply]\\label{lem:supply}\nThe points of $[2^k,2^{k+1})$ with alignment $\\ge g$ to $\\rho_\\sigma$\nform a single residue class modulo $2^g$, of cardinality\n$2^{k-g}\\pm1$; an acyclic orbit consumes them without replacement.\nEvery result of the companion note's supply section (lifetime budgets,\nupcrossing bound) holds verbatim for every phantom root.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\section{The census budget (imported and verified)}\n\n\\begin{lemma}[Affine census; corrected hypothesis]\\label{lem:census}\nLet $x_u=A+Bu$, $\\beta=\\vt(A-\\rho_\\sigma)$, $\\gamma=\\vt(B)$, and\nsuppose $\\gamma\\le\\beta$. Then for $t>\\gamma$,\n\\[\n\\#\\{0\\le u<2^L:\\vt(x_u-\\rho_\\sigma)\\ge t\\}=2^{L-t+\\gamma}.\n\\]\n(Verified: $400/400$ random cases.) The hypothesis $\\gamma\\le\\beta$\ncannot be dropped: for $\\beta<\\gamma$ every $x_u$ has alignment\nexactly $\\beta$ and the count for $t>\\beta$ is $0$, not\n$2^{L-t+\\beta}$ (explicit counterexample: $\\beta=4$, $\\gamma=7$,\n$t=5$, count $0$ vs.\\ $512$).\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Amortized census constant; independent\nrecomputation]\\label{prop:R}\nLet $M(K,\\ell)$ be the number of primitive necklaces of compositions\nof $K$ into $\\ell$ parts and\n\\[\nR=\\sum_{K=3}^{500}2^{-K}\\!\\!\\sum_{\\ell>K/\\log_23}\\!\\!\nM(K,\\ell)\\Bigl(\\log_23-\\frac K\\ell\\Bigr).\n\\]\nOur computation gives $R=0.088236$, confirming the bound $R\\le0.0893$\nof arXiv:2603.11066 (the tail beyond $K=500$ is negligible by the\nChernoff envelope). Against the contraction budget\n$2-\\log_23=0.415$ bits per letter this is a $4.70\\times$ margin.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{remark}\nIn our language: the $K\\le2$ families \\emph{are} the two clocks ---\nthe renewal baseline. \\cref{prop:R} says that all deeper ghost\nchannels combined, weighted by shadow density $2^{-K}$ and per-letter\ngain, supply at most $0.088$ bits of drift advantage: an\n\\emph{amortized} complement to the per-family supply bounds of\n\\cref{lem:supply}. The orbit-level application (that a single orbit\ncannot beat this amortization) is precisely the calibration wall; the\nstructural sum itself is unconditional arithmetic.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\section{The burst--gap calculus is the two clocks}\n\nThe manuscript's coordinates translate exactly onto ours: its run\nlength $k=\\vt(n+1)$ is our $-1$ depth $g$; its ``bursts'' are our\n$1$-runs, its ``gaps'' our descent blocks; its modular laws\n($k\\sim2^{-(j-1)}$, mean $3$; gap $\\sim$Geometric$(\\tfrac12)$, mean\n$2$) are our natural meter laws; its mean-gap threshold\n$g_*\\approx1.71$ is our crossing constant $1/\\log_2(3/2)=1.7095$; and\nits two open hypotheses (mean gap $\\ge g_*$; mean burst $\\le2$) are\nliterally the two channels of our joint calibration conjecture.\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Their Persistent Exit Lemma is the parity\nhandoff]\\label{prop:pel}\nFor $x\\equiv27\\pmod{32}$ (depth-$2$ run end in the persistent class):\nthe run ends in one $b{=}1$ step at $y$ with $h=\\vt(y-1)=3$\n\\emph{exactly}, whence by the $+1$ clock a single $b{=}2$ letter\nfollowed immediately by a new $1$-run --- ``gap length exactly one.''\n(Verified: $4000/4000$.) Proof: $x=32s+27$ gives\n$y=48s+41$, $y-1=8(6s+5)$ with $6s+5$ odd, so $h=3$; $h$ odd is\nprecisely the odd-parity handoff of the companion note.\n\\end{proposition}\n\nIts compatible-class census also reconciles: enumerating odd residues\nmodulo $2^{2D+3}$ whose first $D$ letters lie in $\\{1,2\\}$ gives\nexactly $2^{2D+2}(3/4)^D$ classes (verified $D\\le8$), i.e.\\ density\n$(3/4)^D$ --- their $|C_D|=2\\cdot3^{D-1}$ is the same density in a\ncoarser modulus convention.\n\n\\section{Refutation: the shadow return-time theorem}\n\nThe manuscript asserts (its Thm.~on shadow return times) that after an\norbit exits the depth-$K$ shadow of a family of length $\\ell$, it\ncannot re-enter for $\\ell+K-1$ steps, the proof resting on the claim\nthat $2$-adic alignment recovers at most one bit per step. Both fail:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Measured over $400$ random orbits per family, with visits\ncounted ride-aware (a maximal run of consecutive ridden blocks is one\nvisit): minimum exit-to-re-entry gap $=1$ for all three families\ntested ($\\sigma=(1,2),(1,1,2),(2,1)$), with $17$--$24\\%$ of gaps below\n$K-1$.\n\\item Alignment is not $1$-Lipschitz along orbits: one-step alignment\n\\emph{gains} of $+13$, $+15$, $+16$ bits were observed for\n$\\rho=-1,-19/11,-5$ respectively. (The mechanism: a step maps\nalignment-to-$\\rho$ to alignment-to-the-preimage-root\n$\\rho^{-b}=(2^b\\rho-1)/3\\ne\\rho$, so no per-step monotonicity\nexists.)\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\noindent The correct statement available is supply-side, not\nspeed-side: re-entries at depth $g$ consume the band pool of\n\\cref{lem:supply} without replacement; nothing constrains \\emph{how\nsoon} the next pool point is hit, only \\emph{how many times} it can\never be.\n\n\\section{Position}\n\nThe manuscript's Route-B core, the Carry Independence Conjecture ---\nevery $n_0>1$ eventually takes a letter $b\\ge3$ --- is the extreme\nspecial case $\\mathbb P[h\\text{ odd}]\\equiv1$ of our joint calibration\nconjecture (its Cantor set $\\mathcal C_\\infty$ consists of the orbits\nall of whose $+1$-handoffs are odd forever). The balanced\n(bounded-discrepancy) core of $\\mathcal C_\\infty$ is already excluded\nunconditionally by repetition rigidity and the narrow-band corollary\n($\\sup_j|S_j|<1.246$), which the manuscript leaves conditional. Its\nterminal wall --- ``distributional-to-pointwise promotion'' --- is our\nquenched calibration wall, reached independently. What we import,\nafter verification: the universal repulsion mechanism\n(\\cref{thm:clock}), the census lemma (\\cref{lem:census}, with the\nrestored hypothesis), and the amortized constant\n$R=0.0882$ (\\cref{prop:R}); what we contribute back: the exact ride\ncap, the supply bounds replacing the false return-time theorem, and\nthe Lean-verified rigidity underlying the narrow-band exclusion.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{chang} E.~Y.~Chang, \\emph{Exploring Collatz Dynamics with\nHuman-LLM Collaboration}, arXiv:2603.11066v6 (2026).\n\\bibitem{twoclock} J.~A.~Janik, \\emph{Two Clocks: the $\\pm1$ meters,\ndepth supply, and unconditional escape bounds}, companion note (2026).\n\\bibitem{nb} J.~A.~Janik, \\emph{Narrow-band exclusion and the\n$(D,\\varepsilon)$ phase diagram}, companion note (2026).\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/shadow_frontier/notes/ghostclock.tex"}